\mt1 The Third book of Kingdoms
\c 8
\v 1 When[, after twenty years,] Solomon had finished building the House of the Lord [and his own house, then King Solomon] he assembled all the elders of Israel in Zion, to bring the Ark of the Covenant of the Lord up from the city of David. that is, from Zion.
\v 2 [in the month of Ethanim,]
\v 3 and the priests took up the Ark
\v 4 and the Tent of Witness, and the holy vessels that were in the Tent of Witness.
\v 5 The king and all Israel were in front of the Ark, [sacrificing sheep and oxen without number,]
\v 6 and the priests brought the Ark to its place in the shrine of the House, in the Holy of Holies, right under the wings of the Cherubim,
\v 7 so that the Cherubim spread out their wings over the place of the Ark, so that the Cherubim overshadowed the Ark and the holy things.
\v 8 [The sacred [poles] projected so that the ends of the sacred [poles] were seen from the holy places in front of the shrine, but they were not visible from outside.]
\v 9 There was nothing in the ark except the two tablets of stone which Moses had put there in Horeb, the tablets of the covenant {from the Lord} [which the Lord had made with the children of Israel when they went out from the land of Egypt.]
\v 10 What happened when the priests came out of the holy place was that the cloud filled the House,
\v 11 and the priests could not remain to offer service because of the cloud, for the glory of the Lord filled the House.\p
\p   [verses 12-21]
\p
\v 22 Solomon stood before the altar of the Lord in the presence of all the congregation of Israel and stretched out his hands towards heaven,
\v 23 and he said, ‘Lord God of Israel, there is no God like you in heaven above, or on the earth beneath. You keep covenant and mercy with your servant who walks before you with all his heart,
\v 24 as you did toward your servant David my father, for what you have said with your mouth you have fulfilled it with your hands to this day.
\v 25 And now, Lord God of Israel, keep for your servant David my father what you promised him, saying, “There shall not be taken away from you a man to sit before me on the throne of Israel for as long as your children take heed to their ways, walking before me as you have walked before me.”
\v 26 And now, Lord God of Israel, I pray you, let your promise to David my father be confirmed.
\v 27 But shall God indeed dwell with men upon the earth? If heaven and the heaven of heavens are not sufficient for you, how much less this house which I have built in your name.
\v 28 Yet you, Lord God of Israel, will respect my supplication and listen to the prayer which your servant makes to you in your presence this day,
\v 29 so that your eyes might be open towards this house day and night, towards the place of which you said, “My name shall be there” so that you might listen to the prayer which your servant prays in this place day and night.
\v 30 And you will listen to the prayer of your servant and of your people Israel when they pray towards this place, and you will hear in your dwelling place in heaven, and you shall act and be gracious.’
\c 17
\v 1 Elijah the prophet, the Tishbite from Tishbe in Gilead, said to Ahab, ‘As the Lord God of hosts lives, the God of Israel before whom I stand, there will be no dew or rain during these years, except at my word.’
\v 2 And the word of the Lord came to Elijah, saying,
\v 3 ‘Go eastward from here, and hide by the Wadi Cherith that faces the Jordan.
\v 4 You will be able to drink water from the wadi, and I shall command the ravens to feed you there.’
\v 5 Elijah obeyed word of the Lord. He settled by the Wadi Cherith facing the Jordan,
\v 6 and the ravens brought him bread in the morning and meat in the evening, and he drank water from the wadi.
\v 7 Some time later the wadi dried up because no rain fell on the land.
\v 8 The word of the Lord came to Elijah, saying,
\v 9 ‘Rise, and go from here to Zarephath in the land of Sidon. I have commanded a widow woman there to feed you.’
\v 10 And he set out and went to Zarephath, to the gate of the city. A widow woman was there gathering sticks. Elijah called to her, and said, ‘Fetch me a little water in a cup so that I may drink.’
\v 11 She went to fetch it, and Elijah shouted after her, and said, ‘Bring me a piece of bread in your hand.’
\v 12 The woman said, ‘As the Lord your God lives, I have no bread but only a handful of flour in the jar and a little oil in a jug. I am gathering two sticks and I shall go home, bake it for me and my children, and then we shall eat it and die.’
\v 13 Elijah said to her, ‘Have courage; go home and do as you have said, but first make me a little loaf and bring it out to me. After that make some for youself and your children,
\v 14 for the Lord says this, ‘The jar of flour will not fail, nor the jug of oil grow less, until the day when the Lord gives rain on the earth.’
\v 15 The woman went and did this, and she and he and her children ate.
\v 16 The jar of flour did not fail, and the oil in the jug did not grow less, just as the Lord said when he spoke through Elijah.
\v 17 Some time later it happened that the son of the woman, the mistress of the house, fell ill. His illness grew worse until there was no breath in him,
\v 18 and she said to Elijah, ‘What business have I with you, man of God? Have you come to remind me of my sins, and to kill my son?’
\v 19 Elijah said to the woman, ‘Give me your son.’ He took him from her arms and carried him to the upper room where he lived, and he laid him on his bed.
\v 20 Elijah cried aloud and said, ‘Alas, Lord, the witness of the widow with whom I lodge is that you have done evil to her by killing her son.’
\v 21 He breathed upon the child three times and called upon the Lord, saying, ‘O Lord my God, let this child’s soul return to him again.’
\v 22 And it was so. The child cried aloud,
\v 23 and he brought him down from the upper room into the house and gave him to his mother. Elijah said, ‘See, your son lives.’
\v 24 The woman said to Elijah, ‘Now I know that you are a man of God and that the word of the Lord in your mouth is true.’
\c 18
\v 1 Much later, after three years, the word of the Lord came to Elijah, saying, ‘Go and appear before Ahab, and I shall give rain upon the face of the earth.’
\v 17 When Ahab saw Elijah he said to him, ‘Are you the one who is corrupting Israel?’
\v 18 And Elijah said, ‘I am not corrupting Israel; it is you, and your father’s house, in that you abandoned the Lord your God, and went after the Baals.
\v 19 Now send and gather all Israel to me on Mount Carmel, with the four hundred and fifty prophets of Baal, and the four hundred prophets of the groves who eat at Jezebel’s table.’
\v 20 Ahab sent for all Israel, and gathered all the prophets to Mount Carmel,
\v 21 [and Elijah approached them,] and Elijah said to them, ‘How long will you limp on both knees? If the Lord is God, follow Him; but if it is Baal, follow him.’ [But the people answered him not a word.]
\v 22 And Elijah told the people, ‘I alone am left, the only prophet of the Lord, while there are four hundred and fifty prophets of Baal and four hundred prophets of the groves.
\v 23 Let them give us two bulls, and let them choose one for themselves, and cut it in pieces, and lay it on the wood, but do not set fire to the wood; and I shall prepare the other bull, but light no fire.
\v 24 Then you will call on the name of your god, and I shall call on the name of the Lord my God, and the God who answers with fire, he is God.’ And all the people replied, ‘What you have is said is good.’
\v 25 Elijah said to the prophets of shame, ‘Choose for yourselves one bullock and prepare it first, for you are many, and call on the name of your god; but light no fire.’
\v 26 They took the bullock and prepared it, and called on the name of Baal from early morning until noon, saying: ‘Hear us, O Baal, hear us.’ But there was no sound, there was nothing to be heard; and they ran up and down on the altar which they had made.
\v 27 At midday Elijah the Thesbite mocked them, and said, ‘Call in a louder voice, for it may be your god is meditating, or perhaps he is engaged in business, or else asleep and needs waking.’
\v 28 And they cried with a louder voice, and, according to their custom, they cut themselves with knives and lances until the blood flowed over them.
\v 29 They continued to prophesy until evening, and when it was time for the sacrifice to be offered,  Elijah the Thesbite spoke to the prophets of the abominations, saying, ‘Stand aside for the present, so that I may offer my  burnt sacrifice.’ [And they stood aside and departed.]
\v 30 Elijah said to the people, ‘Draw near to me,’ and all the people drew near to him.
\v 31 And Elijah took twelve stones according to the number of the tribes of Israel, as the Lord had told him, saying, ‘Israel will be your name.’
\v 32 And he built an altar with the stones in the name of the Lord, and he repaired the altar that had been destroyed, and made a channel that could hold two measures of seed around the altar.
\v 33 Then, after placing the wood upon the altar he had built, he cut the burnt offering into pieces and laid them on top of the wood on the altar. He said, ‘Bring me four jars of water and pour it over the burnt offering and over the wood.’ And they did so.
\v 34 He then said, ‘Do it a second time,’ and they did it a second time. He said, ‘Do it a third time,’ and they did it a third time.
\v 35 The water flowed all around the altar, and the channel was filled with water.
\v 36 And Elijah cried out to heaven, saying, ‘Lord God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel, answer me, O Lord, answer me this day with fire, and let all the people know that you are the Lord, the God of Israel, and that I am your servant, and that it is for your sake that I have done these things;
\v 37 [answer me, O Lord, answer me with fire, and let this people know that you are the Lord God,] and that you have turned back the heart of this people.’
\v 38 Then fire fell from the Lord out of heaven, and consumed the whole burnt offering, and the wood, and the water that was in the channel; and the fire licked up the stones and the dust.
\v 39 And all the people fell on their faces, saying, ‘Truly the Lord is God, he is God.’
\v 40 Elijah said to the people, ‘Seize the prophets of Baal; let none of them escape.’ And they seized them. Elijah took them down to the Wadi Kisson and slew them there.
\v 41a And Elijah said to Ahab, Go on your way, eat and drink,
{\v 44b before the rain overtakes you. }
\v 42b Elijah went up to Mount Carmel, and bowed down to the ground with his face between his knees;
\v 45 and the heavens grew dark with clouds and wind, and there came a great rain. And Ahab returned to Jezreel.
\c 19
\v 1a Ahab told his wife Jezebel all that Elijah had done,
\v 2b and Jezebel sent to Elijah, saying, ‘Tomorrow I will make your life as the life of one of these.’
\v 3 Elijah was afraid; he set off and fled for his life. He arrived at Beer-sheba in the land of Judah where he left his servant,
\v 4 but he himself went a day’s journey into the wilderness, and sat down under a juniper tree. With respect to his life, he begged that he might die, saying, ‘Enough, Lord; take my life from me, for I am no better than my fathers.’
\v 5 And there he lay down and slept under the tree. Then someone touched him, and said to him, ‘Arise and eat.’
\v 6 Elijah looked, and saw that there was an oatcake and a flask of water. He got up and ate and drank; then, going back, he lay down.
\v 7 The angel of the Lord came again and touched him and said to him, ‘Arise and eat, or the journey will be too far for you.’
\v 8 And he got up and ate and drank, and went in the strength of that food for forty days and forty nights to Mount Horeb,
\v 9 where he went into a cave and rested. Then the word of the Lord came to him, and said, ‘What are you doing here, Elijah?’
\v 10 And Elijah said, ‘I have been very jealous for the Lord Almighty, for the children of Israel have deserted you; they have taken down your altars and killed your prophets with the sword. I alone am left, and they seek my life, to take it.’
\v 11 He replied, ‘You are to go out tomorrow and stand before the Lord on the mountain. You will see the Lord pass by.’ Then a mighty and powerful wind tore the mountain and shattered the rocks in the presence of the Lord, but the Lord was not in the wind; and after the wind, an earthquake, but the Lord was not in the earthquake;
\v 12 and after the earthquake a fire, but the Lord was not in the fire; and after the fire the sound of a gentle breeze.
\v 13 When Elijah heard it he wrapped his face in his cloak, and went out and stood in the opening; and a voice came to him, saying, ‘What are you doing here, Elijah?’
\v 14 And Elijah said, ‘I have been very jealous for the Lord Almighty, for the children of Israel have abandoned your covenant, thrown down your altars and killed your prophets with the sword, and I alone am left, and they seek my life, to take it.’
\v 15 The Lord said to him, ‘Go on your way; return by the desert road to Damascus. You are to anoint Hazael as King of Syria;
\v 16 and you are to anoint Jehu the son of Nimshi as King of Israel; and you are to anoint Elisha the son of Shaphat to be prophet in your place.’
\v 19a,c When Elijah found Elisha, the son of Saphat, he was ploughing with oxen; and as Elijah passed by him, he threw his cloak over him.
\v 20 Elisha left the oxen and ran after Elijah, and said, ‘I will kiss my father, and then follow you.’ And Elijah said, ‘Go back. What have I done for you?’
\v 21 He went back, and took a yoke of oxen and slaughtered them. He boiled them with the oxen’s tackle, and gave it to the people, and they ate. He then set out and went after Elijah, and ministered to him.
